### When is the permits clause optional?

The `permits` clause may be omitted when every permitted direct subtype is declared in the same source file (the same compilation unit) as the sealed type. The compiler infers the permitted set automatically.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// All subtypes in the same file: permits clause omitted
public sealed interface TransactionResult {
    // Compiler infers: permits Approved, Declined, Pending
}

record Approved(BigDecimal amount) implements TransactionResult {}
record Declined(String reason) implements TransactionResult {}
record Pending(String referenceId) implements TransactionResult {}
```

This compiles exactly as if you had written `permits Approved, Declined, Pending`.

### How does the compiler infer the permitted set?

The compiler reads the entire source file and finds all types that directly extend or implement the sealed type. These become the inferred permitted set. Adding a fourth subtype in the same file automatically extends the set.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// Adding Reversed here automatically includes it in the permitted set
public sealed interface TransactionResult {}

record Approved(BigDecimal amount) implements TransactionResult {}
record Declined(String reason) implements TransactionResult {}
record Pending(String referenceId) implements TransactionResult {}
record Reversed(String txnId, String reason) implements TransactionResult {}
```

### Does inference work for nested types?

Yes. Inference covers both auxiliary top-level classes in the file and nested types.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public sealed interface Account {
    // Nested records: compiler infers permits
    record SavingsAccount(String id, BigDecimal balance)
        implements Account {}
    record CurrentAccount(String id, BigDecimal balance)
        implements Account {}
}
```

### When does permits become mandatory?

The moment any permitted subtype is in a different file, the `permits` clause is required. You cannot mix inference and explicit listing.

```java
// File: TransactionResult.java
package com.bank.model;

// Approved is in another file, so permits is MANDATORY
public sealed interface TransactionResult
    permits Approved, Declined, Pending {}

record Declined(String reason) implements TransactionResult {}
record Pending(String referenceId) implements TransactionResult {}
```

```java
// File: Approved.java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public record Approved(BigDecimal amount) implements TransactionResult {}
```

### What about the final, sealed, or non-sealed requirement?

Each permitted subtype must carry one of three modifiers regardless of whether `permits` is written:

- `final` – cannot be further subclassed (records are implicitly final).
- `sealed` – can be extended, but only by its own permitted set.
- `non-sealed` – reopens the hierarchy below this type.

```java
package com.bank.model;

// All in same file, permits omitted
public sealed interface Account {}

final class SavingsAccount implements Account {
    // final: no further subclasses
}

non-sealed class CurrentAccount implements Account {
    // non-sealed: anyone can extend CurrentAccount
}
```

### Is the bytecode the same with and without permits?

Yes. The inferred permitted set is recorded in the `PermittedSubclasses` class-file attribute identically to an explicit `permits` clause. At the bytecode level there is no difference. The sealing guarantee is identical.

```bash
# Inspect the class file attribute
javap -v com.bank.model.TransactionResult

# Output includes:
# PermittedSubclasses:
#   com/bank/model/Approved
#   com/bank/model/Declined
#   com/bank/model/Pending
```

### How does exhaustive switch work with inferred permits?

Exhaustive `switch` works exactly the same. The compiler uses the inferred permitted set for exhaustiveness checking.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.*;

public class ResultHandler {
    public static String handle(TransactionResult result) {
        return switch (result) {
            case Approved a  -> "Approved: " + a.amount();
            case Declined d  -> "Declined: " + d.reason();
            case Pending p   -> "Pending: " + p.referenceId();
            // No default: exhaustive even with inferred permits
        };
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Same-file inference is a source-level convenience for small algebraic-data-type-style models. It does not weaken the sealing guarantee. For large hierarchies split across files for readability, use explicit `permits`. The key benefit is that adding a new subtype in the same file automatically includes it in the permitted set and causes every non-exhaustive switch to fail compilation, keeping the safety guarantee intact.
